-- title:
--   Locally quasi-finite morphisms have zero-dimensional stalk fibres
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes (in a fixed universe) and let $g \colon X \to Y$ be a morphism which is locally quasi-finite, and let $x$ be a point of $X$. Write $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ for the stalk of the structure sheaf of $X$ at $x$, $\mathcal{O}_{Y,g(x)}$ for the stalk of $Y$ at the image point $g(x)$ under the underlying map of spaces, and let $\mathcal{O}_{Y,g(x)} \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ be the induced map on stalks. The assertion is that the quotient of $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ by the ideal generated by the image of the maximal ideal of the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{Y,g(x)}$ along this stalk map, that is the fibre ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}/\mathfrak{m}_{g(x)}\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$, has Krull dimension equal to $0$ (equality of elements of the extended value type in which `ringKrullDim` takes its values, so the statement also records that this ring is nonzero). No Noetherian or finiteness hypothesis on $Y$ is imposed beyond local quasi-finiteness of $g$.
--
--   This is the local-algebra expression of the fact that a locally quasi-finite morphism has fibres that are zero-dimensional at each point: the fibre ring of $\mathcal{O}_{Y,g(x)} \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ is Artinian, hence of dimension zero. It supplies the zero-dimensional-fibre input to the quasi-finite form of miracle flatness, and is used in the proofs that a locally quasi-finite endomorphism of a smooth connected scheme is flat, that a locally quasi-finite morphism which is smooth of a given relative dimension is finite, flat and surjective, and in the flatness of multiplication-by-$n$ on the relative group law over a field.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_LocallyQuasiFinite_ringKrullDim_stalk_quotient_eq_zero.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
